WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.900
Many programs need a random value in a bounded range: an index, a retry slot, a selection from a list, or a shuffled position.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.960
Turning random bytes or integers into a range is not only a formatting step.

3
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.260
The mapping can introduce bias if it is done carelessly.

4
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:23.700
Simple modulo reduction can bias results when the source range is not evenly divisible by the target range.

5
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.600
Some outputs become slightly more likely than others.

6
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:33.820
The impact depends on the use case, but where fairness or security matters, biased mapping is a real design issue.

7
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:39.540
Rejection sampling is a common conceptual approach for unbiased bounded values.

8
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:47.960
The idea is to accept only source values that map evenly into the target range and retry when the value falls outside that acceptable region.

9
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:54.380
The exact implementation should come from a reviewed helper or approved library pattern rather than improvised at each call site.

10
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.780
Range functions should validate that the requested range is meaningful and non-empty.

11
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:07.480
A helper should handle edge cases such as zero range, inverted bounds, maximum values, and type conversions deliberately.

12
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.580
These checks are separate from the strength of the underlying random source.

13
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:18.660
Shuffling and selection logic should use unbiased index generation where fairness or security matters.

14
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:23.000
Code review should check range mapping separately from random byte generation.

15
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:29.980
A good source can still be weakened by poor mapping, and a careful mapping cannot make a weak source suitable for sensitive values.
